    <!-- The Deep "Why" Architectural Justifications -->
    <div class="card shadow-sm border border-primary border-top border-3 mb-4">
        <div class="card-header bg-light py-2 fw-bold text-primary">
            <i class="bi bi-question-circle-fill me-2"></i> The Deep "Why" Behind Architectural Decisions
        </div>
        <div class="card-body p-3 small text-muted">
            <div class="row g-3">
                <div class="col-md-6">
                    <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded border h-100">
                        <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark"><i class="bi bi-cpu-fill text-primary me-1"></i> Why Blazor Server (SignalR) over WebAssembly?</h6>
                        <p class="mb-0">
                            <strong>The Problem:</strong> Blazor WebAssembly compiles IL into browser WebAssembly, downloading a 15–30 MB runtime and easily consuming 400–600 MB of browser RAM. On a 2013 laptop or mobile tablet, it is sluggish.<br />
                            <strong>The Choice:</strong> Blazor Server runs locally in .NET 10, consuming only ~140 MB of memory total. UI diffs are streamed over a persistent 2 KB SignalR binary socket, achieving &lt;50ms latency.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="col-md-6">
                    <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded border h-100">
                        <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark"><i class="bi bi-box-fill text-warning me-1"></i> Why a Custom ER7 Parser over NHapi?</h6>
                        <p class="mb-0">
                            <strong>The Problem:</strong> <code>NHapi</code> is a 15-year-old port of Java HAPI. It brings over 40 MB of monolithic assemblies for HL7 versions 2.1 through 2.8 with verbose Java-style factories.<br />
                            <strong>The Choice:</strong> A custom 100-line C# pipe-and-hat parser teaches developers the actual delimiter rules (<code>|</code>, <code>^</code>, <code>~</code>, <code>\</code>, <code>&amp;</code>) cleanly, with zero external DLL bloat.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="col-md-6">
                    <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded border h-100">
                        <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark"><i class="bi bi-hdd-network-fill text-success me-1"></i> Why In-Memory Processing (Zero Local DB)?</h6>
                        <p class="mb-0">
                            <strong>The Problem:</strong> Installing SQL Server Express or Docker containers on low-end hardware causes severe CPU spikes and disk thrashing.<br />
                            <strong>The Choice:</strong> HL7 v2 and FHIR are <em>wire transfer protocols</em> (data in flight). In-memory models make this repo 100% portable: any engineer can clone and run it immediately with zero database migration steps.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="col-md-6">
                    <div class="p-3 bg-light rounded border h-100">
                        <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark"><i class="bi bi-file-earmark-medical-fill text-info me-1"></i> Why Docs-as-Code (In-App Living Documentation)?</h6>
                        <p class="mb-0">
                            <strong>The Problem:</strong> External wikis (Confluence, SharePoint) get out of sync with code within months.<br />
                            <strong>The Choice:</strong> Documentation lives as version-controlled Markdown files inside <code>Docs/</code>. Using Markdig, team members can read, search, and copy verified docs directly inside the web app.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

